Design Troubleshooting and Decision Support › 1. Start from the failure mode, not the CAD command

Translate “this design feels wrong” into one or more observable problems:
- interference / collision;
- weak or flexible panel;
- impossible bend sequence;
- hole/slot distortion near a bend;
- inaccessible fastener or tool;
- door/gasket compression inconsistency;
- water/dust ingress path;
- assembly stack-up mismatch;
- excessive part count or welding;
- hard-to-inspect datum scheme;
- cost driven by process rather than material.

Design Troubleshooting and Decision Support › 3. Option format

For each concept state:
- what changes;
- which problem it addresses;
- new risks introduced;
- manufacturing impact;
- drawing/CAD changes required;
- what must be verified before release.

Design Troubleshooting and Decision Support › 4. Common sheet-metal rescue patterns

Use as **patterns**, not automatic answers:
- add return flange/bead/rib instead of only increasing thickness;
- move or resize bend-adjacent features;
- split a collision-prone one-piece body into controlled subassemblies;
- use tabs/slots or locators to control weld assembly;
- change fastener direction or captive hardware for service access;
- isolate gasket compression from weld distortion;
- add drip/labyrinth geometry so sealing is not dependent on a single edge;
- change datum selection to match functional interfaces and inspection setup.

Tolerance Stack, Fits and Functional Clearances › 4. Sheet-metal realities

- bend angle/radius variation can convert into large edge-position error over long flanges;
- formed features may require profile/position concepts rather than very tight ± dimensions;
- welding adds distortion and fixture dependence;
- coating thickness affects tight fits, grounding points and gasket interfaces;
- laser-cut accuracy does not imply bent-assembly accuracy.

Enclosure Thermal Management › 1. Required inputs

- internal dissipated heat by component and duty cycle;
- maximum/minimum ambient temperature;
- allowable internal temperature and component derating limits;
- enclosure dimensions/material/color/location (indoor/outdoor, sun exposure);
- target IP/IK and contamination environment;
- altitude and airflow restrictions;
- available ventilation, heat exchanger or cooling options.

Enclosure Thermal Management › 4. IP-aware patterns

- filtered fans require a filter/grille system compatible with the target enclosure concept;
- vents/breathers are components with their own ratings and installation constraints;
- place inlet/outlet to create a real flow path, not a short circuit;
- avoid drain paths that bypass the protected boundary;
- maintain service access for filter replacement;
- do not call an assembly IP-rated because individual components have IP markings—final assembly verification still applies.

Manufacturing Process Selection and DFMA › 4. DFMA review

Look for:
- unnecessary unique thicknesses/material grades;
- mirrored parts that can be commonized;
- features requiring special punches/tools without volume justification;
- welds that exist only because locating features are absent;
- inaccessible nuts/screws;
- hardware inserted too early for coating;
- cosmetic surfaces exposed to grinding/handling;
- tolerances tighter than the functional need;
- drawings that mix process instructions with design requirements ambiguously.

Image to CAD Geometry Reconstruction › Pipeline

1. preprocess raster;
2. detect lines/circles;
3. de-duplicate geometry;
4. infer geometric relations (parallel/perpendicular/horizontal/vertical/concentric);
5. optionally calibrate pixels to mm using a known reference;
6. validate basic geometry/manufacturing limits supplied by the user;
7. export JSON/SVG/DXF;
8. review in CAD before release.

Image to CAD Geometry Reconstruction › Scale hierarchy

Best to worst:
1. dimension explicitly printed on a drawing;
2. calibration target in the same plane;
3. known component with verified size in the same plane;
4. multiple consistent references;
5. perspective-corrected estimate;
6. no reference → proportions only, no mm claim.

Requirements, Risk and Engineering Release › 2. Lightweight design-risk review

For each important requirement capture:
- failure mode;
- effect;
- likely causes;
- current prevention/detection;
- proposed action;
- owner/status.

The repository does not prescribe a universal risk score because company FMEA schemes differ. Use the organization’s method when one exists.

Requirements, Risk and Engineering Release › 4. Release gates

A production release should confirm at minimum:
- material/thickness/finish;
- interfaces and critical dimensions;
- bend model/tooling source where flat size depends on it;
- weld/fastener definition;
- drawing standard and revision;
- DXF layer/entity review;
- ingress/thermal claims phrased correctly;
- unresolved deviations approved;
- BOM and quotation assumptions aligned with the released design.

Metrology, Inspection and Quality Planning › Characteristic classes

Separate:
- safety/regulatory or interface-critical characteristics;
- function-critical dimensions;
- process-control dimensions;
- reference/information-only dimensions.

This helps avoid inspecting every dimension with the same effort.

Engineering Sanity Checks and Calculation Discipline › Common AI calculation failures

- mixing mm and m;
- confusing margin and markup;
- applying 90° bend shortcuts at other angles;
- treating K-factor as a material constant;
- summing tolerances by RSS without a statistical basis;
- using air-flow heat balance while ignoring pressure drop/recirculation;
- turning image-derived estimates into manufacturing dimensions.
